
6p-Block Quadruple-Zeta Basis Set Archive
=========================================

Title: Relativistic quadruple-zeta and revised triple-zeta and double-zeta
       basis sets for the 4p, 5p and 6p elements.

Author: Kenneth G. Dyall

Journal: Theor. Chem. Acc., to be submitted

Date: 25 Apr 2005

When publishing work that uses these basis sets, please use the following citation:

K.G. Dyall, in preparation. Available from the Dirac web site, http://dirac.chem.sdu.dk.


This archive contains the exponents and the SCF spinor or orbital coefficients, 
given with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
the basis sets described in the above article. The exponent sets reported include
the valence correlating functions. In the optimizations, the outermost two d
functions were replaced by the three valence correlating d functions and the
remaining d functions reoptimized; likewise the two correlating f functions were
added to the SCF set and the SCF functions reoptimized. In all the following 
descriptions, this is the set referred to as the SCF exponent set.

The archive is set out as follows.

1. Exponents and SCF coefficients for Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave functions, in order
   of Z. The large component functions are given first, followed by the small 
   component functions. Both components are labelled with the large component
   symmetry type, but the primitive functions for the small component are
   those obtained by restricted kinetic balance from the large component.
   The spin-orbit components have a "-" label for j=l-1/2 and a "+" label for
   j=l+1/2. 

2. Exponents and SCF coefficients for spin-free modified Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave
   functions, in order of Z. The large component functions are given first, 
   followed by the pseudo-large component functions, then the Foldy-Wouthuysen
   transformed large component functions. Note that all components have the same
   symmetry type. 

3. Exponents of diffuse s, p, d and f functions.

4. Exponents of valence correlating functions.

5. Exponents of core correlating functions.



Basis set construction.
----------------------

Several different types of basis sets may be constructed by adding primitive 
functions. These basis sets are described below. 

NOTE: Functions are counted from the smallest exponent. 

1. Valence (equivalent to cc-pvqz). To the SCF occupied set, add the following:
   1st, 2nd, and 4th s primitives from the SCF set 
   3rd, 4th, and 5th p primitives from the SCF set
   1st, 2nd, and 3rd d primitives from the SCF set (correlating 3d)
   1st and 2nd outer f primitives from the SCF set (correlating 2f)
   valence correlating 1g functions.

   Note: the outermost p primitives are not uncontracted. An alternative is 
   to use the 1st, 3rd, and 4th p primitives or 2nd, 3rd, and 4th p primitives, 
   which results in a loss of up to 1mEh correlation energy for Rn. However,
   when the core correlating functions are added, this loss is made up. 

   (HF + 3s3p3d2f1g valence)

2. Core-valence. To basis set 1, add the following:
   5th, 6th, and 8th s primitives from the SCF set 
   6th, 7th, and 8th p primitives from the SCF set
   5th, 6th, and 7th d primitives from the SCF set
   3rd, 4th, and 5th f primitives from the SCF set
   core correlating 2g1h functions.

   Note: the optimum p primitives are 4th, 5th, and 6th for Tl and Pb, and
   5th, 6th, and 8th for Bi - Rn. However, the 4th and 5th are already in
   the valence set. An alternative is to add just the 6th and 8th to the 
   valence set if the addition of the 6th, 7th and 8th is problematic. 

   An alternative core-valence $p$ set is to use the 1st, 3rd, 4th,
   5th, 6th, and 8th p primitives. This set covers the optimum core and
   valence correlation and allows better for valence flexibility. 

   (HF + 3s3p3d2f1g valence + 3s3p3d3f2g1h core)

3. Diffuse basis: add the diffuse s, p, d, f and g functions to any of the above
   basis sets.





